From: ps5cemu <ps5cemu@localhost>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 19:32:20 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] PS5: the Divine Beasts' backdrop gets one shared view and
 each eye's projection from the background pass's own block; built, untested
 on console

The console session on c271758 showed the per-eye path in 0053 never ran: its
prepare hook was gated on the layer's special camera/projection pair, and the
outside world is drawn by the model scene, so the layer gate returned before
the view scan and only the pose line was logged. The record-side pose change
did reach the background camera, but the draw's view block was left as the
record's mono/combined matrices, which is why the backdrop scaled with yaw,
slid against the deck and could pass in front of deeper interior geometry.

This replaces the layer gate with the model scene's own view array. At record,
the hook keeps the background camera's own position, composes the gameplay yaw
with the head's middle rotation and leaves that shared view, the projection's
depth rows and its near/far in a small record. At replay, for each display
list and each eye, the hook finds the view array entry whose view is the
background camera's (views 0 and 3-6 are the main camera and shadow cascades),
takes that index's per-view uniform block, and swaps the eye's shared matrices
for the shared background view and that eye's projection: the eye's horizontal
and vertical rows, the background projection's depth rows. recalcBackgroundGpuView
then writes that one block, keeping the frame's shadow and environment uniforms,
the previous-view cache and the view array as they are for the main camera.
The two eyes share one viewpoint and differ only in projection, so the backdrop
sits at infinity and stays behind real geometry as in flat play. No draws,
passes or readbacks are added; the overworld never has a background camera, so
its path is unchanged.

Probes, all gated to a Divine Beast entry: one entry line with the caller LR,
near/far/FOV and the shared pose; one line comparing the shadow context's block
slot 0 with the recorded view; one line saying whether the view array/block was
found and whether the block already held the background view; and at most one
summary line per five seconds with the written/prepared counts per eye.
